WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.820
This steak isn't actually steak.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:07.800
It's made of beetroot; seaweed;&nbsp;
pea protein, and algae.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.500
And it's fresh off the printer...

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:21.279
Just outside Barcelona in the Catalonian Farmland,&nbsp;you'll find the meatiest of restaurants.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.280
El Santuari is renowned for its extensive&nbsp;
range of exotic meats.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:27.020
Wagyu

00:00:27.294 --> 00:00:27.794
Crocodile

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:28.450
Zebra

00:00:28.593 --> 00:00:29.233
Kangaroo

00:00:29.371 --> 00:00:33.905
And now 3D printed&nbsp;vegan steaks, cutlets, and fillets.

00:00:45.933 --> 00:00:47.675
I am Giuseppe Scionti.

00:00:47.779 --> 00:00:52.349
I am a&nbsp;CEO and founder at Nova Meat, and I am a bioengineer by background.

00:00:52.581 --> 00:00:57.261
Josep and I we only have a name in common because he's called Josep in Catalan and I'm Giuseppe in Italian.

00:00:57.652 --> 00:00:59.378
But the rest, we come from different worlds.

00:00:59.378 --> 00:01:04.640
He's a meat lover, I'm trying to solve the meat issue, and find an alternative to that.

00:01:25.172 --> 00:01:29.711
As a child I was interested both in&nbsp;the nature world, dreaming about the Amazon rainforest,

00:01:29.711 --> 00:01:34.772
and also my grandfather was an&nbsp;inventor and it was some kind of a model to me

00:01:34.772 --> 00:01:40.680
so I was watching cassettes about Da Vinci&nbsp;and other inventors and scientists of the past.

00:01:42.274 --> 00:01:44.739
Giuseppe is the brains behind Nova Meat

00:01:44.739 --> 00:01:47.731
and they're not making your average vegan burgers.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:55.972
His background in tissue engineering&nbsp;helped him develop a 3D printer that&nbsp;recreates the taste, texture and aroma&nbsp;of real meat.

00:01:56.085 --> 00:01:58.733
 It even reacts like meat&nbsp;on the grill.

00:01:58.896 --> 00:02:06.000
Microfilaments are ordered in&nbsp;a very specific way so that it recreates&nbsp;the structure and the function of an animal&nbsp;muscle.

00:02:06.276 --> 00:02:11.145
We move that big machine and we&nbsp;are able to do it at that scale of hundreds&nbsp;of kilograms per hour afterwards.

00:02:11.762 --> 00:02:12.905
[Music]

00:02:30.333 --> 00:02:36.076
I remember when we tried the steak the first&nbsp;time I was very worried that he was working with tough cuts 

00:02:36.163 --> 00:02:38.439
and we were working with just&nbsp;a prototype

00:02:38.728 --> 00:02:40.521
but he was very excited.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.000
[Music]

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.560
But then he gave us very interesting inputs so&nbsp;that we could improve these products very much.

00:03:10.655 --> 00:03:11.655
[Music]

00:03:24.327 --> 00:03:27.880
In my opinion, it is the most straightforward way&nbsp;to save the planet 

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.480
and this is my&nbsp;mission from now until I die.

00:03:32.509 --> 00:03:36.000
This unlikely pair might not share all the same&nbsp;values...

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.555
but they are always happy to share a meal.

00:03:43.404 --> 00:03:43.904
[Music]

